## Solution

**Claim:** The Zariski closure of $X = \{(x, \sin x) : x \in \mathbb{A}^1\}$ in $\mathbb{A}^2$ is all of $\mathbb{A}^2$.

**Proof.**

We work over $k = \mathbb{R}$ or $\mathbb{C}$ (any field of characteristic zero on which $\sin$ is defined). The Zariski closure $\overline{X}$ is the common zero locus of all polynomials $p \in k[x,y]$ that vanish on $X$:

$$I(X) = \{p \in k[x,y] : p(x, \sin x) = 0 \text{ for all } x \in k\}.$$

We show $I(X) = \{0\}$, which gives $\overline{X} = V(0) = \mathbb{A}^2$.

**Key fact:** $\sin x$ is transcendental over $k(x)$.

*Proof of key fact.* Suppose for contradiction that $\sin x$ is algebraic over $k(x)$. Then it satisfies a minimal polynomial

$$f(x)^n + a_{n-1}(x) f(x)^{n-1} + \cdots + a_0(x) = 0$$

with $a_i(x) \in k(x)$, not all zero, and $n \geq 1$ minimal. Clearing denominators, we may assume $a_i(x) \in k[x]$. Evaluating at any $x_0$ with $\sin(x_0) = 0$ gives $a_0(x_0) = 0$. But $\sin x$ vanishes at infinitely many points ($x = m\pi$ for every $m \in \mathbb{Z}$, all distinct in characteristic $0$). Hence $a_0(x)$, a polynomial, has infinitely many roots, so $a_0 \equiv 0$. This contradicts the minimality of $n$ (we could divide out a factor of $\sin x$). Therefore $\sin x$ is transcendental over $k(x)$. $\square$

Now let $p \in k[x,y]$ with $p(x, \sin x) \equiv 0$. Write

$$p(x, y) = \sum_{j=0}^{N} q_j(x)\, y^j, \qquad q_j(x) \in k[x].$$

Then

$$0 = p(x, \sin x) = \sum_{j=0}^{N} q_j(x)\, (\sin x)^j.$$

This is a polynomial relation in $\sin x$ over $k(x)$. Since $\sin x$ is transcendental over $k(x)$, every coefficient $q_j(x)$ must be identically zero. Hence $p = 0$.

Therefore $I(X) = \{0\}$, and

$$\overline{X} = V(I(X)) = V(0) = \mathbb{A}^2.$$

$\blacksquare$

